---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../../styles/pages/work-project.css';
import Citation from '../../components/Citation.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="La Mer en Corps — Laurie Di Francesco"
  description="La Mer en Corps — water and dreams, giving body to movement. A year of passages."
  image="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
>

  <!-- ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="phero">
    <div class="phero-bg" aria-hidden="true">
      <img
        src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
        alt=""
        loading="eager"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
    <div class="phero-inner container">
      <p class="phero-eyebrow">Tracing</p>
      <h1>La Mer<br /><strong>en Corps</strong></h1>
      <p class="phero-caption">Water and dreams, giving body to movement.<br />A year of passages.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Vidéo (2/3) + citations Bachelard (1/3) ──────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-vid-intro rv">
    <div class="lmec-vid-col">
      <div class="lmec-video-wrap">
        <video
          muted
          loop
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          poster="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps-cover.jpg"
        >
          <source src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps/lamerenCorps.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
        </video>
        <div class="lmec-video-overlay" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="lmec-play-icon" viewBox="0 0 48 48" fill="none">
            <circle cx="24" cy="24" r="23" fill="rgba(20,32,43,0.28)"/>
            <circle cx="24" cy="24" r="23" stroke="rgba(255,255,255,0.5)" stroke-width="1"/>
            <polygon points="18,13 36,24 18,35" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.88)"/>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <button class="lmec-fullscreen-btn" aria-label="Full screen">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M4 8V4h4M20 8V4h-4M4 16v4h4M20 16v4h-4"/>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="lmec-text-col">
      <div class="epi-wrap">
        <blockquote class="epi-justify"><span class="k">The sea</span> is not a body one sees, nor even a body one embraces. It is <span class="k">a dynamic medium that responds to the dynamics of our offences</span>.</blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="epi-wrap">
        <blockquote class="epi-justify lmec-bq-long">If the gaze of things is a little gentle, a little grave, a little pensive, it is a gaze of water. Examining imagination leads us to this paradox: in the imagination of generalised vision, water plays an unexpected part. <span class="k">The true eye of the earth is water. In our eyes, it is water that dreams.</span> Are our eyes not "that unexplored pool of liquid light that God has placed deep within us"? <span class="k">In nature, it is still water that sees, still water that dreams.</span></blockquote>
        <blockquote class="epi-justify lmec-bq-short"><span class="k">The true eye of the earth is water. In our eyes, it is water that dreams.</span></blockquote>
        <cite>Gaston Bachelard — L'Eau et les Rêves <span class="epi-tradfr">freely translated from the French</span></cite>
      </div>
      <hr class="horizon epi-horizon" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Texte du projet — pleine largeur ─────────────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-prose rv">
    <p class="opening-eyebrow">The Project</p>
    <p class="opening-text"><span class="k"><em>En Corps à la Mer</em> ended where the body found water again. <em>La Mer en Corps</em> begins one year later, at the other end of that gesture: fifty-three swims along the coast, from August 2025 to July 2026.</span></p>
    <p class="opening-text">A body that had given way, returning to the same water until something in it changed shape. Not healing. Something closer to what a person tells themselves when they are not yet well but cannot afford to collapse: nothing is final. <span class="k">You repeat the movement to hold yourself up, and one day the movement and the water hold you up in return.</span></p>
    <p class="opening-text">In concert, musician Benjamin Clementine stops the music and has the room repeat that line back to him, until the sentence carries the ones who carry it.</p>
    <Citation author="Benjamin Clementine" justify>
      Repetition is not always about saying the same thing twice. Sometimes it is about returning to a wound until it changes shape.
    </Citation>
    <p class="opening-text">Close to fifty thousand arm strokes. Water from fourteen degrees to twenty-seven. A heart at one hundred and thirty-seven. The year is recorded by a watch on my wrist, one point per second: latitude, longitude, temperature, pulse.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Texte avec photo flottante ──────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="lmec-prose rv">
    <figure class="lmec-photo-float">
      <img src="/images/work/la-mer-en-corps/GP010207.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>underwater, Mediterranean sea</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="opening-text">This is what interests me here. Swimming takes everything: no free hand, no free eye, no distance from the subject. <span class="k">Many times I find myself immersed in a deep blue, the exact same blue as the sky on a pure, beautiful day: my landmarks and my sense of direction vanish.</span></p>
    <p class="opening-text">So the recording is handed over. On one side, the most connected tools available: a satellite constellation, a sensor on my wrist, an algorithm that turns a year of positions into a drawing. On the other, the oldest and the most makeshift: a roll of film, a camera sealed inside a waterproof phone pouch, tied to the buoy. Neither gives me control. The first records without me; the second lets in salt, glare, and whatever the swell decides.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">What comes back is data, pixels, exposed silver, and most of the time blind attempts, before any of it is a photograph. <span class="k">I have stopped treating that blindness as a failure. It is a condition of the work: at that distance from the shore, with both arms taken, chance is not what ruins the image, it is what makes it.</span> Turning any of this back into an image means passing through every tool that made it, from the algorithm to the developing tank. I did not want to keep any of them out of the frame.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">Superimposed, the fifty-three traces no longer describe a place. Each is brought back to its own centre; the shoreline disappears and only the gesture remains. What glows brightest is what was repeated most.</p>
    <p class="opening-text">The year closes on a loop. In music, a loop is a repeating section of sound played continuously without a noticeable break. In the sea, it looks like a figure that ends on the shore where it began. But we never come back from a swim the same: the body that finished this year is not the body that started it. <span class="k">Seen from the side, the loop was a climbing spiral all along.</span></p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── Projet suivant ───────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="next-project container">
    <span class="next-label">Next project</span>
    <a href="/work/toward-the-blue">Toward the Blue →</a>
  </div>

</Layout>

<script>
  const obs = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in'); obs.unobserve(e.target); }
    });
  }, { threshold: .06 });
  document.querySelectorAll('.rv').forEach(el => obs.observe(el));

  const vid     = document.querySelector('.lmec-video-wrap video') as HTMLVideoElement | null;
  const overlay = document.querySelector('.lmec-video-overlay') as HTMLElement | null;
  const fsBtn   = document.querySelector('.lmec-fullscreen-btn') as HTMLElement | null;

  // Source verticale (reel 9:16) sur mobile, avant tout chargement
  if (vid && window.matchMedia('(max-width: 700px)').matches) {
    const src = vid.querySelector('source') as HTMLSourceElement | null;
    if (src) src.src = '/images/work/la-mer-en-corps/lamerenCorps-mobile.mp4';
  }

  if (vid && overlay) {
    const showStart = Date.now();
    vid.addEventListener('playing', () => {
      const delay = Math.max(0, 2500 - (Date.now() - showStart));
      setTimeout(() => overlay.classList.add('hidden'), delay);
    }, { once: true });

    vid.load();
    vid.play().catch(() => {});
    overlay.addEventListener('click', () => {
      overlay.classList.add('hidden');
      vid.play().catch(() => {});
    });
  }
  if (fsBtn && vid) {
    fsBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (vid.requestFullscreen) vid.requestFullscreen();
      else if ((vid as any).webkitRequestFullscreen) (vid as any).webkitRequestFullscreen();
    });
  }
</script>
